<section id="consolidated-legacy-evidence" class="legacy-consolidations"><h2>Consolidated OBD adapter comparisons: retained source evidence</h2><p>Adjacent pairwise device comparisons are now organized in one protocol, platform and app-fit resource. The decision evidence and selected original diagrams below are retained from each study. Full original files remain available in the repository history; their public URLs redirect here to avoid maintaining competing indexed pages.</p><article class="legacy-case" id="obdlink-cx-vs-lx"><h3>OBDLink CX vs LX: BLE for iPhone or Classic Bluetooth?</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Research from the former <code>/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-lx/</code> comparison, now redirected to this consolidated resource.</p><h4>Protocol scope comes after pairing</h4><p>LX is the general legislated-OBD-II model. CX is BMW-oriented and carries manufacturer-documented network exclusions. Neither name guarantees every manufacturer module, service function or proprietary network.</p>
<h4>The application and vehicle are separate gates</h4><p>Hardware transport cannot override a missing application approval. Check the current OBDLink model documentation, the intended app support page and the exact vehicle selector together. Record the phone operating system and app version because a model can be approved on one host path but not another.</p>
<h4>Put firmware and existing hardware into total cost</h4><p>Compare the authentic purchase source, firmware-update route and every application license required. If the buyer already owns a supported Android device and general OBD app, LX may avoid unnecessary platform changes. An iPhone or BMW-first workflow can make CX the lower-friction path even before list prices are compared.</p><details><summary>View original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-lx/cs034-visual-a-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-lx/cs034-visual-a.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs LX: BLE for iPhone or Classic Bluetooth?: preserved comparison figure 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 1; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-lx/cs034-visual-b-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-lx/cs034-visual-b.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs LX: BLE for iPhone or Classic Bluetooth?: preserved comparison figure 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 2; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article class="legacy-case" id="obdlink-cx-vs-mx-plus"><h3>OBDLink CX vs MX+: Match the Adapter to Your Car and Apps</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Research from the former <code>/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-mx-plus/</code> comparison, now redirected to this consolidated resource.</p><h4>Protocol coverage in practical terms</h4><p>The extra protocol rows matter only when they intersect the vehicles the adapter must serve. CX covers the ISO protocols OBDLink lists for its BMW-focused role. MX+ adds SAE J1850 PWM and VPW plus the Ford MS-CAN and GM SW-CAN networks identified in the official matrix. Those additions are not abstract “more compatibility”: they are the reason a buyer with older Ford or GM vehicles should not assume CX is a discounted substitute.</p>
<h4>BLE, Windows and platform fit</h4><p>Both adapters have Android, iOS and Windows paths, but the connection architecture is not identical. CX uses Bluetooth Low Energy. OBDLink's matrix qualifies its Windows support with a compatible BLE adapter, so a desktop or laptop buyer must verify that hardware rather than reading “Windows” as a complete setup. MX+ uses its listed Bluetooth connection path without that CX-specific requirement.</p>
<h4>Compatibility is a three-party promise</h4><p>The vehicle, adapter and app must agree. BimmerCode’s adapter list lists both CX and MX+ for all series in its selector and calls CX made for BimmerCode. That supports adapter compatibility; it does not prove every coding option exists on every BMW/MINI or that either adapter behaves identically in other apps.</p><details><summary>View original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-mx-plus/cs009-fit-matrix-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-mx-plus/cs009-fit-matrix.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs MX+: Match the Adapter to Your Car and Apps: preserved comparison figure 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 1; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-mx-plus/cs009-adapter-decision-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-mx-plus/cs009-adapter-decision.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs MX+: Match the Adapter to Your Car and Apps: preserved comparison figure 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 2; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article class="legacy-case" id="obdlink-cx-vs-unicarscan-ucsi-2100"><h3>OBDLink CX vs UniCarScan UCSI-2100 for BimmerCode and BimmerLink</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Research from the former <code>/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-unicarscan-ucsi-2100/</code> comparison, now redirected to this consolidated resource.</p><h4>Start with the app-to-car chain</h4><p>Coding and diagnostics run through a chain: the app, your phone's platform, the adapter, the Bluetooth connection, and finally your exact vehicle. Every link has to line up. Both adapters clear the first four links for most owners; the last link — your specific series and year — is the one that varies, so treat the app's compatibility page as the authority.</p>
<h4>Identify the exact models</h4><p>The OBDLink CX is a Bluetooth Low Energy adapter built by OBD Solutions and marketed specifically for BimmerCode and BimmerLink. The UniCarScan UCSI-2100 is a Bluetooth 4.0 (also Low Energy) adapter that BimmerCode lists as recommended and that also serves other apps such as MotoScan for BMW motorcycles and TuneECU. Same core job, wireless BMW coding and diagnostics; different breadth of ecosystem.</p>
<h4>Confirm app support and platform</h4><p>According to BimmerLink, both adapters work over Bluetooth on iOS and Android for all BMW series, which puts them on equal footing for the core apps. The practical difference is the breadth of the app ecosystem: the UniCarScan is also recommended for BMW-motorcycle work through MotoScan and for motorcycle ECU tuning through TuneECU, while the OBDLink CX stays focused on the BMW car apps. Note that MotoScan and the current version of TuneECU are Android apps, so that motorcycle ecosystem lives on Android — the UniCarScan is also listed as working with Windows PCs for some apps, but treat that as a secondary detail, not a current reason to buy, since the motorcycle apps that distinguish it are Android.</p><details><summary>View original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-unicarscan-ucsi-2100/cs-084-compat-map-desktop.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs UniCarScan UCSI-2100 for BimmerCode and BimmerLink: preserved comparison figure 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 1; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><picture><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-unicarscan-ucsi-2100/cs-084-adapter-matrix-desktop.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs UniCarScan UCSI-2100 for BimmerCode and BimmerLink: preserved comparison figure 2" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 2; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure></details></article>
<article class="legacy-case" id="obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-bm-plus"><h3>OBDLink CX vs vLinker BM+: Choose by App and Vehicle</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Research from the former <code>/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-bm-plus/</code> comparison, now redirected to this consolidated resource.</p><h4>Verdict first</h4><p>Choose OBDLink CX when you want the adapter explicitly positioned for BMW use, detailed public protocol and power documentation, and Bluetooth Low Energy connectivity on a supported host. Choose vLinker BM+ when it appears as supported for your exact BimmerCode or BimmerLink vehicle selection and its price or availability is preferable.</p>
<h4>Why the app selector outranks adapter folklore</h4><p>BimmerCode's adapter page is not a single universal whitelist. It is a selector shaped by vehicle series and platform. Its own manual and support material repeatedly make vehicle equipment and control-unit availability part of the boundary. That means “supported by BimmerCode” is the start of the check, not the end.</p>
<h4>OBDLink CX: where its documentation is strongest</h4><p>OBDLink describes CX as a Bluetooth Low Energy product designed around BMW applications. Its current notes say it is BLE-only, not Bluetooth Classic, and list the protocol families it supports. The company's comparison guide also distinguishes CX from broader OBDLink models intended for other vehicle networks or general-purpose coverage.</p><details><summary>View original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-bm-plus/cs028-compatibility-layers-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-bm-plus/cs028-compatibility-layers.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs vLinker BM+: Choose by App and Vehicle: preserved comparison figure 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 1; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure>
