<article class="legacy-case" id="obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-mc-plus"><h3>OBDLink CX vs vLinker MC+: Pick the Exact App Path</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Research from the former <code>/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-mc-plus/</code> comparison, now redirected to this consolidated resource.</p><h4>Start with the named application</h4><p>CX and MC+ are wireless interfaces, not capability licenses. Begin with the exact application, phone platform, vehicle generation and intended task. A supported radio link cannot supply a function that the application or vehicle selector does not offer.</p>
<h4>Firmware, seller and ownership checks</h4><p>Buy from a source where model identity, return rights and vendor support are traceable. Save the order record, photograph the received label and use the documented firmware process. These steps matter because similarly named adapters and stale marketplace pages can make a correct comparison fail in practice.</p>
<h4>Compatibility evidence note</h4><p>Adapter, platform and application claims were checked against the cited manufacturer documentation on August 29, 2026. Verify the exact vehicle, phone, app version, adapter revision and firmware path before purchase. No latency, current-draw or vehicle-coverage measurement was performed.</p><details><summary>View original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-mc-plus/cs036-visual-a-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-cx-vs-vlinker-mc-plus/cs036-visual-a.svg" alt="OBDLink CX vs vLinker MC+: Pick the Exact App Path: preserved comparison figure 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 1; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure>
