<article class="legacy-case" id="obdlink-mx-plus-vs-lx"><h3>'OBDLink MX+ vs LX: Choose by Platform and Vehicle Network'</h3><p class="legacy-provenance">Research from the former <code>/guides/obdlink-mx-plus-vs-lx/</code> comparison, now redirected to this consolidated resource.</p><h4>What all legislated OBD-II protocols does not mean</h4><p>Standard OBD-II primarily covers regulated emissions-related data and trouble codes. It does not promise access to every manufacturer module or service function. OBDLink's MX+ page explicitly adds Ford MS-CAN and GM SW-CAN; LX's page tells buyers to choose MX+ when those networks are required.</p>
<h4>App compatibility controls the purchase</h4><p>OBDLink's compatible-app directory marks support per app, platform and adapter. For example, an iOS app may list MX+ but not LX because LX does not support iOS. Android applications can support both while exposing different vehicle-specific functions.</p>
<h4>Ownership and safety</h4><p>Both adapters advertise automatic sleep/wake, firmware updates and protected pairing. Use OBDLink's current firmware procedure for the exact model and platform. Do not update firmware during an unstable power or connection state.</p><details><summary>View original technical diagrams (2)</summary><figure><picture><source media="(max-width:640px)" srcset="/images/guides/obdlink-mx-plus-vs-lx/cs016-platform-protocol-mobile.svg"/><img src="/images/guides/obdlink-mx-plus-vs-lx/cs016-platform-protocol.svg" alt="'OBDLink MX+ vs LX: Choose by Platform and Vehicle Network': preserved comparison figure 1" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture><figcaption>Original adapter analysis diagram 1; confirm current model and application-level evidence before use.</figcaption></figure>
